\zlabel{triptych:concise:chronology:start}
% Draft uses unedited generated chronology definitions in the parent scratch directory.
% Geography below is limited to accepted canonical contexts. Additional historical
% authorship/audience detail requires independent source inspection in the new run.
\section*{Scriptural Date and Location}
\begin{dossiertable}
\dossierentry{Gradual}{}{Ps.~32 (33):6,12}{Creation and the people chosen by the Lord.}{\chronodate{gradual}{\chronologyannotation{gradual}}}{The whole psalm praises creation, divine counsel and mercy; armies do not secure salvation. Its appointed verses identify neither a writing-place nor one historical occasion. The displayed composition bound is a Psalter-wide critical horizon, not an individual date.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierentry{Communion}{}{Ps.~75 (76):12--13}{God's judgment; gift-bearers around the Lord.}{\chronodate{communion}{\chronologyannotation{communion}}}{The psalm places failed military power and the rescue of the meek before vows and gifts. The appointed conclusion supplies no secure date or writing-place for its speaker. Augustine's inward reading and Bellarmine's emphasis on mortal rulers explain its reception, not its composition date.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierentry{Alleluia}{}{Ps.~101 (102):2}{An afflicted speaker; Sion and gathered peoples.}{\chronodate{alleluia}{\chronologyannotation{alleluia}}}{Personal frailty opens toward Sion's restoration in the full psalm. Sion is the object of hope, not an established writing-place. The general composition bound does not date a particular restoration.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierentry{Introit}{}{Ps.~118 (119):1,124,137}{A servant learning to walk God's law.}{\chronodate{introit}{\chronologyannotation{introit}}}{The liturgical antiphon places acknowledgment of justice before the mercy petition, reversing their order in the complete psalm. No particular crisis, first audience or writing-place is established by the appointed verses. The date is the shared Psalter boundary.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierentry{Offertory}{}{Dan.~9:17--19, adapted}{Daniel praying for sanctuary and named people.}{\chronodate{offertory}{\chronologyannotation{offertory}}}{The full chapter reads Jeremiah's promise, confesses communal failure and asks restoration. The seventy-year statement is a duration within that historical setting, not a composition date. The chant selects and rearranges petitions; its liturgical form has a distinct history.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierevententry{Gospel}{}{Matt.~22:34--46}{Jesus, the lawyer and gathered Pharisees.}{\chronodate{gospel}{\chronologyannotation{gospel}}}{Event: the love-command exchange follows the dispute about resurrection; Jesus then asks about David's Son and Lord (Matt.~22:23--46).}{The corpus preserves received composition alternatives and supplies no narrated-event date at these loci. Composition, first audience and narrated encounter must remain distinct; this draft adds no unsupported writing-place or calendar date.}
\dossierseparator
\dossierentry{Epistle}{}{Eph.~4:1--6}{Paul, a prisoner, addressing the called community.}{\chronodate{epistle}{\chronologyannotation{epistle}}}{The appeal for humble, patient unity proceeds to different ministries and the speech, work and forgiveness of common life. The displayed traditional composition alternatives do not by themselves establish the prison city or a complete modern authorship account.}
\end{dossiertable}
\zlabel{triptych:concise:chronology:end}
